Result: 125 passed, 0 failed, 0 errors across all four aggregated modules
(pureKafka 28, purerestlib 45, inventoryService 23, orderService 29).
scalafmtCheck clean.

What this proves:
- Tracing: KafkaTracing.injectHeaders/traced correctly propagate W3C trace
  context through Kafka message headers - a consumer span created via
  traced() is confirmed (via otel4s's in-memory trace testkit) to be a
  child of the producer span whose headers it read, and is tagged with
  OpenTelemetry's messaging.system/messaging.destination.name semantic
  attributes. No hand-rolled TextMapPropagator needed - otel4s's own
  Tracer.propagate/joinOrRoot already resolve an implicit carrier codec
  for Map[String, String], the same mechanism purerestlib's HTTP
  ClientTracing/ServerTracing already rely on.
- Consumer lag: ConsumerLag.record/fromConsumer read Kafka's own
  already-tracked per-partition records-lag metric (via
  KafkaConsumer.metrics(), confirmed against the real fs2-kafka 3.6.0 and
  kafka-clients 3.8.1 APIs directly, not guessed) and republish it as an
  otel4s gauge (purekafka.consumer.lag) tagged by
  topic/partition/consumer-group. fs2-kafka 3.6.0 has no endOffsets method
  at all - confirmed by checking its real API before assuming otherwise.
- Structured logging: audited every log line Phases 1-3 emit. Only
  DeadLetter.guard actually had Kafka-specific context (topic/key) in
  scope to retrofit - Retry/CircuitBreaker (fully generic F[A]) and
  StreamSupervisor (generic over Stream[F, O]) have no per-record context
  available at all. DeadLetter's log line now carries topic/dlq_topic/key
  as structured ctx instead of string-interpolated. No eventType/
  correlationId extraction was added anywhere in this module - that
  requires decoding a service-specific payload, which stays the calling
  service's job per this module's own non-functional requirements
  (confirmed, not assumed, by re-reading spec.md before starting this
  piece).

This closes Phase 4 of the purekafka track. Remaining work: Phase 5 (load
test proving all of this under sustained load).
